============================================================
1. ISSUE IDENTIFIED
============================================================

A recurring expense was occasionally being generated twice for
the exact same recurrence date.

Example observed:

Expense:
CME, CBOT, COMEX

Amount:
C$11.07

Frequency:
Monthly

Occurrence Date:
2026-09-13

Two identical active expense rows existed for the same:

user_id
+
recurring_group_id
+
expense_date


============================================================
2. ROOT CAUSE
============================================================

The recurring expense generator used an application-level
"check-then-insert" pattern.

Original flow:

1. Check Supabase to see whether an occurrence already exists.
2. If no row exists, insert the occurrence.

This was not concurrency-safe.

Two generator executions could run at nearly the same time:

Generator A                  Generator B
-----------                  -----------

SELECT occurrence            SELECT occurrence
       ↓                            ↓
   NOT FOUND                    NOT FOUND
       ↓                            ↓
   INSERT                      INSERT
       ↓                            ↓
   SUCCESS                     SUCCESS

              ↓
        DUPLICATE ROW


The duplicate records had creation timestamps only milliseconds
apart.

This confirmed that the problem was a race condition between
the existence check and the INSERT.

The application could not guarantee uniqueness by itself.


============================================================
3. DATABASE STATE BEFORE THE FIX
============================================================

The expenses table previously had only its primary-key
uniqueness:

expenses_pkey

PRIMARY KEY:
id

There was no database-level uniqueness protection for:

user_id
+
recurring_group_id
+
expense_date


Therefore PostgreSQL allowed two identical recurring
occurrences to be inserted.


============================================================
4. DUPLICATE DATA AUDIT
============================================================

A database query was used to identify existing duplicate
recurring occurrences:

SELECT
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date,
    COUNT(*) AS occurrence_count
FROM public.expenses
WHERE recurring_group_id IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
ORDER BY
    occurrence_count DESC,
    expense_date;


The audit identified four duplicate occurrence groups.

Affected recurrence dates included:

2026-06-23
2026-08-12
2026-09-04
2026-09-13


The duplicate rows were reviewed and confirmed to be accidental
duplicate generations of the same recurring occurrence.


============================================================
5. HISTORICAL DUPLICATES CLEANED
============================================================

The accidental duplicate rows were removed while retaining one
valid occurrence for each recurring date.

This cleanup was required before creating the database
uniqueness rule.

After cleanup, the duplicate detection query returned:

Success. No rows returned


Therefore there were no remaining duplicate recurring
occurrences in the database at the time the uniqueness index
was created.


============================================================
6. DATABASE-LEVEL FIX
============================================================

A unique index was added to the expenses table:

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX expenses_recurring_occurrence_unique_idx
ON public.expenses (
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date
)
WHERE recurring_group_id IS NOT NULL;


The resulting index was verified directly through pg_indexes:

expenses_recurring_occurrence_unique_idx

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX
ON public.expenses
USING btree
(
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date
)
WHERE recurring_group_id IS NOT NULL


This establishes the database-level invariant:

For a given user:

same recurring_group_id
+
same expense_date
=
only one row may exist.


============================================================
7. IMPORTANT SOFT-DELETE BEHAVIOR
============================================================

The unique index intentionally does NOT exclude:

is_deleted = true


Therefore deleted recurring occurrences remain part of the
uniqueness rule.

This is intentional and matches the Elite X recurring-expense
architecture.

Example:

If an occurrence for:

2026-09-13

is manually deleted:

is_deleted = true


the deleted row remains in the database.

The recurring generator sees that occurrence as already existing
and does not recreate it.

This preserves the existing rule:

DELETED RECURRING OCCURRENCES MUST NOT BE REGENERATED.


============================================================
8. WHY user_id IS INCLUDED
============================================================

The uniqueness identity is:

user_id
+
recurring_group_id
+
expense_date


This explicitly scopes the recurrence occurrence to the user.

This is appropriate for the multi-user Elite X architecture
and prevents one user's recurring data from conflicting with
another user's data.


