============================================================
10. RECURRING VALIDATION IMPROVEMENT
============================================================

The generator already validated:

is_recurring
is_active
is_generated


A new validation was added for:

recurring_group_id


If a recurring expense does not have a recurring_group_id,
generation stops and an error is logged.

Reason:

A recurring occurrence cannot safely be identified without a
recurring group.


============================================================
11. OCCURRENCE LOOKUP IMPROVEMENT
============================================================

The old generator performed an individual database query for
every possible occurrence date.

Old conceptual behavior:

SELECT date 1
SELECT date 2
SELECT date 3
SELECT date 4
...
INSERT date 1
INSERT date 2
...


The new generator retrieves existing occurrence dates in a
single query.

It filters by:

user_id
+
recurring_group_id
+
occurrence dates


The existing dates are then converted into a JavaScript Set.

Missing dates are calculated in memory.

This eliminates the unnecessary N+1 query pattern.


============================================================
12. CONCURRENCY-SAFE INSERT
============================================================

The generator now uses Supabase upsert behavior with:

onConflict:
"user_id,recurring_group_id,expense_date"

and:

ignoreDuplicates:
true


The important behavior is:

If the occurrence does not exist:
    → create it.

If another generator creates it first:
    → ignore the duplicate.

If another generator creates it between our SELECT and INSERT:
    → PostgreSQL unique index prevents a second row.

The application therefore no longer treats concurrent
generation as a duplicate-creation failure.


============================================================
13. IMPORTANT: NO OVERWRITE OF EXISTING OCCURRENCES
============================================================

The generator uses:

ignoreDuplicates: true


It does NOT use normal update-style upsert behavior.

This is important because generated expenses can be manually
edited by the user.

An existing occurrence must never be overwritten simply because
the recurring generator runs again.

Therefore:

Existing occurrence:
    → preserved exactly as it is.

Missing occurrence:
    → created.

Concurrent duplicate attempt:
    → ignored.


============================================================
14. FINAL ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

Recurring generation now follows:

Recurring Anchor
       ↓
Generate required occurrence dates
       ↓
Query existing occurrences
       ↓
Determine missing dates
       ↓
Prepare missing occurrence rows
       ↓
Supabase UPSERT
       ↓
PostgreSQL UNIQUE INDEX
       ↓
--------------------------------
|                              |
New occurrence             Already exists
|                              |
INSERT                     IGNORE
|                              |
--------------------------------
       ↓
Exactly one occurrence


The database is now the final authority for occurrence
uniqueness.


============================================================
15. WHY THE DATABASE CONSTRAINT IS THE REAL FIX
============================================================

The application-level check alone could never guarantee
uniqueness.

Even with:

SELECT → check → INSERT


another request could execute between the SELECT and INSERT.

The database unique index closes this race window.

Therefore the invariant is enforced at the database layer:

ONE USER
+
ONE RECURRING GROUP
+
ONE EXPENSE DATE
=
ONE RECURRING OCCURRENCE


This remains true regardless of how many generator executions,
browser refreshes, tabs, or concurrent requests occur.


